WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
Injection review starts at trust boundaries.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:16.920
In a Java web application, untrusted input can arrive through request bodies, query parameters, route parameters, headers, cookies, JSON payloads, XML payloads, multipart forms, and file uploads.

3
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:31.820
Backend services add more entry points: message queues, webhooks, partner API responses, scheduled imports, environment variables, configuration values, command-line arguments, and data restored from storage.

4
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.440
None of these should be assumed safe just because they are internal.

5
00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:39.800
The important question is not only where data enters, but where it is used.

6
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:52.700
A value may pass through controllers, DTOs, validators, mappers, services, repositories, templates, loggers, and outbound clients before it reaches a sensitive sink.

7
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.200
By the time the risk appears, the original source may be several method calls away.

8
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:03.700
That is why data-flow mapping is more useful than checking only the controller method that receives the request.

9
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.580
A practical review traces values from boundary to sink.

10
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:16.880
Ask where the value came from, whether its intended meaning is narrow, which transformations happen along the way, and whether the final API treats it as data or syntax.

11
00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:32.580
Search terms, filter fields, sort directions, file names, tenant identifiers, callback URLs, XML nodes, and message attributes often deserve special attention because they look ordinary while still influencing interpretation.

12
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.140
Good Java design makes trust boundaries visible.

13
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.960
Use request models that separate raw external input from validated domain values.

14
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.940
Prefer typed enums for choices like sort direction or report format.

15
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:53.480
Convert strings to domain types early, reject ambiguous values, and pass typed values into safe APIs.

16
00:01:53.580 --> 00:02:03.020
When the codebase shows a clear path from external input to constrained internal representation, injection flaws become easier to prevent and easier to review.
